#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Taxonomy node-ID removal guard (t/3851).
#
# Refuses a commit that removes node IDs from taxonomy/Origin/{accelerationist,
# safetyist,skeptic,situations}.json unless the commit message names exactly
# those IDs in a `Taxonomy-Node-Removal:` trailer. Set equality, not presence:
# a copied or stale trailer fails because the sets will not match.
#
# WHY commit-msg AND NOT pre-commit:
#   The acknowledgment is a commit-message trailer, and at pre-commit time the
#   message does not exist yet. commit-msg receives the message file as $1 and
#   the index is still inspectable, so it is the only hook with BOTH halves.
#   (t/3851 originally specified pre-commit; that was a mechanism error.)
#
# WHICH INDEX IT SEES (t/3851, e/243#4):
#   git sets GIT_INDEX_FILE to a TEMP index for `git commit -- <paths>` and to
#   .git/index for a bare commit. `git show :<path>` honours it, so this hook
#   sees exactly what will be committed -- a peer's unrelated staged removal is
#   invisible during a pathspec commit. Verified both paths.
#
# WARN-ONLY for now -- prints and exits 0. Per Gate Promotion it stays
# advisory for >=1 green cycle against the real repo, and the flip to blocking
# (exit 1) is gated on a mandatory Second Opinion (e/243). DO NOT flip without
# it. Flip = set WARN_ONLY=0 below and update this header's status line.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE FLIPPING -- what the warn-only cycle will NOT have proven
# (t/3851, e/243#10):
#   Organic taxonomy traffic is ADDITION-ONLY. Measured: 18 consecutive commits
#   at monotonic growth (204 -> 223 nodes), zero removals; an earlier cycle was
#   +5/0. So a green warn-only cycle from real traffic exercises the
#   NO-REMOVAL EARLY-EXIT path -- which was never in doubt -- and says nothing
#   about the refusal path being promoted. It satisfies ">=1 green cycle
#   against the real target" in letter, not in substance.
#   The blocking behaviour IS proven offline: 15 arms x both WARN_ONLY settings
#   (below), both index paths, through real `git commit`. What no real-data run
#   has yet exercised is a refusal against the actual 1429-node corpus.
#   If you want that before flipping, it is cheap and writes nothing:
#     git clone --depth 1 file://<data-repo> <tmp>/guard-probe
#     cp this file + taxonomy_node_removal_verdict.py into <tmp>/.githooks/
#     cd <tmp> && git config core.hooksPath .githooks && set WARN_ONLY=0 HERE
#     drop one node from taxonomy/Origin/accelerationist.json, stage, commit
#       -> expect REFUSAL and no new SHA; then confirm a legitimate commit in
#          the same clone still succeeds (otherwise "nothing happened" cannot
#          be told apart from a broken hook).
#   A CLONE, not a worktree: core.hooksPath lives in .git/config, which
#   worktrees SHARE -- flipping WARN_ONLY in a worktree flips it for the shared
#   checkout too.
#   Status 2026-10-03: probe designed (e/243#10) and NOT run; PI chose to wait
#   for organic traffic instead. This annotation exists so the flip is not made
#   on the assumption that an organic green covered the refusal path.
#
#   BUT DO NOT READ THE ABOVE AS "FLIPPING IS RISKY" (e/243#12). The evidence
#   is incomplete; the risk is low, because this guard's failure direction is
#   benign. A WRONG REFUSAL blocks a legitimate commit and the author sees a
#   named node, a paste-ready trailer, the whole-index discriminator and
#   collateral counts -- worst case they reach for --no-verify. Cost: one
#   confused author and a loud signal. It does not cost data. The failure this
#   guard exists for runs the OTHER way -- a deletion passing silently, which
#   is what WARN_ONLY=1 permits today and what the flip closes.
#   So the first organic REMOVAL after the flip is itself the refusal test,
#   conducted safely, because a wrong refusal is self-explaining. Waiting for
#   traffic is a defensible sequence, not a gap papered over; it is slower to
#   full evidence with the asymmetry pointing the right way throughout.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT env-overridable (e/243#10): making WARN_ONLY settable from
# the environment to ease testing would add a bypass quieter than --no-verify
# to a guard whose value is being hard to satisfy by accident. The clone above
# gives the same testability with no production surface.
#
# GV status: WARN-ONLY, promotion-ready pending the SO sign-off below.
#   Predicate: 7/7 arms (taxonomy_node_removal_verdict.py); shared trailer
#         parser acknowledged_ids 7/7 (t/3870#5: the push-side re-check imports it).
#   Hook: 15 arms x BOTH WARN_ONLY settings = 30/30 (arms 13-15: UTF-8 BOM in the
#         staged copy, at HEAD, and on both sides -- proven to discriminate against
#         the pre-fix hook, where arm 14 let a removal through; t/3851#11-12).
#         Every arm driven through a
#         real `git commit` (NOT a manual hook call -- a manual invocation reads
#         as a full-index commit and so covers only one of the two real paths;
#         that gap is what made the first six-arm set single-path. e/243#4.)
#   Index paths: bare/full-index AND pathspec, which now have DIFFERENT correct
#         behaviours -- arm 9 (pathspec, own removal) must NOT print the
#         whole-index warning that arm 2 (bare) must print.
#   Arm 8: a peer's staged removal is invisible to a pathspec commit (the
#         committed tree keeps all 3 nodes) -- no cross-agent false positive.
#   Arm 11: a REACHABILITY arm, not a behaviour arm. It asserts the hook can
#         never see an UNMERGED watched file, which is what licenses this hook
#         to ignore `U` (git aborts first: "Committing is not possible because
#         you have unmerged files" / "cannot do a partial commit during a
#         merge"). If a future git runs hooks there, arm 11 fails and the
#         omission becomes a real gap. A `U` branch was written and then
#         REMOVED on proving it unreachable -- it would have been dead code
#         that read in review as covering the hazard (the Class-8 shape).
#   Real repo: 1429 nodes HEAD == 1429 index, reason=no-removals, 0 false
#         positives; edges.json 16.6 MB / 34,284 edges parses.
#   Arm set: scratchpad gv_3851_v3.sh (reseeds from a fixed SHA and asserts each
#         arm actually staged something -- an earlier version silently no-op'd
#         two arms with "nothing to commit" and read as passing).
#
# Origin: acc-intentions-003 was deleted with 194 edges on 2026-09-30 and was
# caught only incidentally, by a guard looking for something else.
#
# Override: git commit --no-verify  (skips EVERY hook, including the
#           rationale-drop guard in pre-commit -- prefer the trailer)
#
# Install: git config core.hooksPath .githooks   (once per checkout)
#
# EXECUTION RECORD (t/3851, same shape as situations-bdi, t/3892#6): every run that
# reaches a verdict appends one JSON line, hook "node-removal", to
# <common git dir>/hook-telemetry.jsonl (AI_TRIAD_HOOK_TELEMETRY overrides it), with
# result skip|pass|violation|unverified, action allowed|refused, ms, worktree, index
# and HEAD. Without it the warn cycle cannot be read: a pass prints nothing and a
# WARNING goes only to whoever ran the commit, so a dead guard and a passing one look
# identical (Live-Fire Proof, t/3396). An unexpected crash records `unverified`
# before the traceback. Every append is isolated and can never change the verdict.

set -euo pipefail

WARN_ONLY=1

MSG_FILE="${1:?commit-msg hook requires the message file path}"

T0="$(date +%s%3N 2>/dev/null)"; case "$T0" in ''|*[!0-9]*) T0="$(( $(date +%s) * 1000 ))" ;; esac
record_skip () {  # $1 = detail. The two pre-Python exits; the Python side records the rest.
  { local common log wt now
    common="$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)"
    log="${AI_TRIAD_HOOK_TELEMETRY:-$common/hook-telemetry.jsonl}"
    [ "$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)" = "$common" ] && wt=false || wt=true
    now="$(date +%s%3N 2>/dev/null)"; case "$now" in ''|*[!0-9]*) now="$(( $(date +%s) * 1000 ))" ;; esac
    printf '{"ts":"%s","hook":"node-removal","result":"skip","action":"allowed","warn_only":%s,"ms":%s,"worktree":%s,"index":"%s","head":"%s","detail":"%s"}\n' \
      "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$([ "$WARN_ONLY" -eq 1 ] && echo true || echo false)" \
      "$(( now - T0 ))" "$wt" "$(basename "${GIT_INDEX_FILE:-index}")" \
      "$(git rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)" "$1" >> "$log"
  } 2>/dev/null || true
}

WATCHED=(
  "taxonomy/Origin/accelerationist.json"
  "taxonomy/Origin/safetyist.json"
  "taxonomy/Origin/skeptic.json"
  "taxonomy/Origin/situations.json"
)

STAGED_LIST="$(git diff --cached --name-only)"
HIT=0
for f in "${WATCHED[@]}"; do
  if printf '%s\n' "$STAGED_LIST" | grep -qF "$f"; then HIT=1; break; fi
done
[ "$HIT" -eq 1 ] || { record_skip "no watched taxonomy file in commit"; exit 0; }

git rev-parse --verify HEAD >/dev/null 2>&1 || { record_skip "no HEAD (initial commit)"; exit 0; }

# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE: importing the verdict module would otherwise leave
# .githooks/__pycache__/ as untracked noise in every `git status` of a repo
# whose cleanliness is actively monitored (the t/2452 drift backstop).
PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 - "$MSG_FILE" "$WARN_ONLY" "$T0" <<'PYEOF'
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time

MSG_FILE = sys.argv[1]
WARN_ONLY = sys.argv[2] == "1"
EXIT_BAD = 0 if WARN_ONLY else 1
T0_MS = int(sys.argv[3]) if len(sys.argv) > 3 and sys.argv[3].isdigit() else None


def record(result, detail="", action=None):
    """Append this run's execution record (see the header). Isolated: any failure here
    is swallowed and can never change the verdict."""
    try:
        if action is None:
            action = "refused" if (result in ("violation", "unverified") and not WARN_ONLY) else "allowed"

        def git_out(*args):
            return subprocess.run(["git", *args], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()

        common = git_out("rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
        rec = {
            "ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()),
            "hook": "node-removal",
            "result": result,
            "action": action,
            "warn_only": WARN_ONLY,
            "ms": (int(time.time() * 1000) - T0_MS) if T0_MS else None,
            "worktree": git_out("rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir") != common,
            "index": os.path.basename(os.environ.get("GIT_INDEX_FILE", "") or "index"),
            "head": git_out("rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD"),
            "detail": str(detail).replace("\n", " ")[:300],
        }
        log = os.environ.get("AI_TRIAD_HOOK_TELEMETRY") or os.path.join(common, "hook-telemetry.jsonl")
        with open(log, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(rec, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n")
    except Exception:
        pass


def _record_crash(exc_type, exc, tb):
    # An uncaught exception exits non-zero, so `set -e` fails the commit in EITHER mode:
    # record it as refused, then print the normal traceback. Behaviour is unchanged.
    record("unverified", f"guard crashed: {exc_type.__name__}: {exc}", action="refused")
    sys.__excepthook__(exc_type, exc, tb)


sys.excepthook = _record_crash

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.getcwd(), ".githooks"))
from taxonomy_node_removal_verdict import acknowledged_ids, node_removal_verdict  # noqa: E402

WATCHED = [
    "taxonomy/Origin/accelerationist.json",
    "taxonomy/Origin/safetyist.json",
    "taxonomy/Origin/skeptic.json",
    "taxonomy/Origin/situations.json",
]
EDGES = "taxonomy/Origin/edges.json"
SITUATIONS = "taxonomy/Origin/situations.json"



def err(msg):
    print(msg, file=sys.stderr)


def read_json_at(ref_path):
    """-> (doc, status) with status in {'ok','absent','unparseable'}.
    `git show` yields LF-normalised content, so this is CRLF-safe on Windows.

    Reads BYTES, not text (DevOps, t/3851): json.loads(bytes) detects a UTF-8
    BOM itself, while text=True decodes with the LOCALE encoding, so a BOM
    survives as U+FEFF (or as mojibake under cp1252) and json rejects the file.
    PowerShell writes BOM'd JSON (e.g. afd1aae4). Unhandled, that made a valid
    file 'unparseable', and a BOM'd HEAD copy hit the Q4 repair escape below,
    which SKIPS the file: a node removed in that commit passed unchecked.
    Same decode path as the rationale guard in pre-commit, which was never
    affected for this reason."""
    try:
        raw = subprocess.run(["git", "show", ref_path],
                             capture_output=True, check=True).stdout
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError:
        return None, "absent"
    try:
        return json.loads(raw), "ok"
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return None, "unparseable"


def node_ids(doc):
    nodes = doc.get("nodes") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(nodes, list):
        return None
    return {n["id"] for n in nodes if isinstance(n, dict) and "id" in n}


# Files this commit will actually touch. Respects GIT_INDEX_FILE, so under a
# pathspec commit this is the pathspec, not the whole shared index.
commit_files = subprocess.run(["git", "diff", "--cached", "--name-only"],
                              capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.split()

# Which KIND of commit is this? (e/243#6)
#   pathspec  -> GIT_INDEX_FILE is a temp lock, e.g. .git/next-index-25812.lock
#   bare / -a -> .git/index  (also the default when the var is UNSET, which is
#                why a manually-invoked hook correctly reads as full-index)
# Only a full-index commit can sweep in a peer's staged work, so the
# re-commit-with-a-pathspec advice is shown only there.
_idx = os.environ.get("GIT_INDEX_FILE", "")
FULL_INDEX_COMMIT = (_idx == "") or os.path.basename(_idx) == "index"

# A watched file absent from the index means it is being DELETED wholesale, so
# every node in it really is removed -- which is what the verdict then reports
# (arm 12). Two non-cases, both investigated and both deliberately NOT guarded,
# because a branch for either would be unreachable dead code:
#   - UNMERGED / conflicted path: git aborts the commit BEFORE any hook runs
#     ("Committing is not possible because you have unmerged files"; a pathspec
#     commit gets "cannot do a partial commit during a merge"), so this hook can
#     never observe a `U` entry.
#   - empty / garbage GIT_INDEX_FILE: git reports every file as deleted and
#     committing really would delete them, so "all nodes removed" is TRUE for
#     that input. Git never hands a hook such an index anyway.
head_ids, staged_ids = set(), set()
repairing = []          # watched files unparseable at HEAD but fixed in this commit
for path in WATCHED:
    h_doc, h_st = read_json_at(f"HEAD:{path}")
    s_doc, s_st = read_json_at(f":{path}")

    # Q4 escape (e/243#2): HEAD is corrupt and this commit fixes it -> allow the
    # repair. Corruption being COMMITTED is still refused, below.
    if h_st == "unparseable" and s_st == "ok":
        repairing.append(path)
        continue
    if s_st == "unparseable":
        err(f"[node-removal-guard] COULD NOT VERIFY: staged {path} is not parseable JSON.")
        err("  Refusing rather than guessing. Fix the JSON, or commit the repair on its own.")
        record("unverified", f"staged {path} not parseable JSON")
        sys.exit(EXIT_BAD)

    h_ids = node_ids(h_doc) if h_st == "ok" else set()
    s_ids = node_ids(s_doc) if s_st == "ok" else set()
    if h_ids is None or s_ids is None:
        err(f"[node-removal-guard] COULD NOT VERIFY: {path} has no 'nodes' array.")
        record("unverified", f"{path} has no nodes array")
        sys.exit(EXIT_BAD)
    head_ids |= h_ids
    staged_ids |= s_ids

if repairing:
    print(f"[node-removal-guard] JSON repair detected ({', '.join(repairing)}) — "
          "those files skipped for this commit.")

# Assert our own input shape: reading nothing means the extractor failed, not
# that nothing changed. Never pass on that.
if not head_ids and not repairing:
    err("[node-removal-guard] COULD NOT VERIFY: read 0 node IDs from HEAD. "
        "This is a guard failure, not a clean result.")
    record("unverified", "read 0 node IDs from HEAD")
    sys.exit(EXIT_BAD)

# Shared parser (t/3870#5): the push-side re-check imports the same function,
# so the two layers cannot disagree about what a commit acknowledged.
# utf-8-sig: a message file written by a PowerShell editor may carry a BOM.
with open(MSG_FILE, "r", encoding="utf-8-sig") as fh:
    acked = acknowledged_ids(fh)

v = node_removal_verdict(head_ids, staged_ids, acked)

if v["ok"]:
    if v["reason"] == "acknowledged-removal":
        print(f"[node-removal-guard] OK: {len(v['removed'])} acknowledged removal(s).")
        record("pass", "acknowledged: " + ",".join(sorted(v["removed"])))
    else:
        record("pass", "repair: " + ",".join(repairing) if repairing else "")
    sys.exit(0)

removed = set(v["removed"])


def collateral(ids):
    """Edges + situation linked_nodes referencing the removed IDs. Reported,
    never gated on (e/243#2 Q2) -- the acknowledgment should be INFORMED, and
    the incident's severity was the 194 edges, not the one node."""
    out = []
    doc, st = read_json_at(f":{EDGES}")
    if st == "ok":
        edges = doc.get("edges") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(edges, list):
            out.append("edges.json has no 'edges' array — collateral NOT counted")
        elif not edges:
            out.append("edges.json parsed but held 0 edges — collateral NOT counted")
        else:
            n = sum(1 for e in edges if isinstance(e, dict)
                    and (e.get("source") in ids or e.get("target") in ids))
            out.append(f"{n} edge(s) reference the removed node(s)")
    else:
        out.append(f"edges.json {st} — collateral NOT counted")

    doc, st = read_json_at(f":{SITUATIONS}")
    if st == "ok":
        nodes = doc.get("nodes") if isinstance(doc, dict) else []
        n = sum(1 for s in nodes if isinstance(s, dict)
                and any(x in ids for x in (s.get("linked_nodes") or [])))
        out.append(f"{n} situation(s) link to the removed node(s)")
    else:
        out.append(f"situations.json {st} — collateral NOT counted")
    return out


err("")
if v["unacknowledged"]:
    err(f"[node-removal-guard] {len(v['unacknowledged'])} node ID(s) would be REMOVED "
        "without acknowledgment:")
    for i in v["unacknowledged"]:
        err(f"    {i}")
if v["spurious"]:
    err("[node-removal-guard] trailer names ID(s) that are NOT being removed "
        "(stale or copied trailer):")
    for i in v["spurious"]:
        err(f"    {i}")

if removed:
    err("")
    err("  Collateral this removal also carries:")
    for line in collateral(removed):
        err(f"    {line}")
    err("  (reported, not blocked — acc-intentions-003 took 194 edges with it)")

err("")
err("  If the removal is YOURS and intended, name exactly those IDs:")
err("")
err("      Taxonomy-Node-Removal: " + ", ".join(v["unacknowledged"] or v["removed"]))
err("")

# Shown ONLY for a whole-index commit, the one case that can sweep in a peer's
# staged work. A pathspec commit cannot, so it gets the trailer advice alone
# and is spared a warning that would not apply to it. (e/243#6)
if FULL_INDEX_COMMIT:
    err("  ⚠ This is a WHOLE-INDEX commit — it includes everything currently")
    err("    staged, which on a shared checkout may include another agent's work:")
    for f in commit_files:
        err(f"        {f}")
    err("")
    err("    If the taxonomy change above is NOT yours, do NOT add the trailer —")
    err("    that would certify someone else's deletion into your provenance")
    err("    record. Re-commit with an explicit pathspec instead:")
    err("")
    err("        git commit -m \"...\" -- <your paths>")
    err("")

record("violation", "unacknowledged: " + ",".join(v["unacknowledged"])
       + ("; spurious: " + ",".join(v["spurious"]) if v["spurious"] else ""))

if WARN_ONLY:
    err("[node-removal-guard] WARN-ONLY: commit allowed. Will block once promoted (t/3851).")
    sys.exit(0)

err("[node-removal-guard] BLOCKING: commit rejected.")
sys.exit(1)
PYEOF
